# Database dumps.
#
# ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is not cosmetic. A pattern like
# `supabase/` with no slash matches a directory of that name at ANY depth — so
# it also swallowed `lib/work/supabase/`, the four Supabase client modules the
# entire workspace imports. They were never committed, the local build kept
# passing because the files were on disk, and the first fresh clone (Vercel)
# failed with 21 "Can't resolve '@/lib/work/supabase/server'" errors.
#
# Anchor every directory rule meant for the repo root.
/supabase/
/supabase-shop/

# Work schema. `supabase-work/` used to be ignored wholesale, which silently
# swallowed every migration written after the rule was added — 4 of them, plus
# the dev seed. Migrations are the source of truth for the schema, and
# `npm run work:db:validate` applies them from here, so a fresh clone without
# them cannot verify or rebuild anything.
#
# The directory itself must stay unignored (`/*`, not `/`): git will not
# re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded, so a blanket ignore
# makes the negations below dead rules.
/supabase-work/*
!/supabase-work/migrations/
!/supabase-work/seed.sql
# Generated by `npm run work:db:snapshot` from the migrations. Versioned so the
# current schema is readable in one file and a fresh database can be built from
# it, matching how supabase/ and supabase-shop/ are laid out.
!/supabase-work/schema.sql
!/supabase-work/storage.sql

# Unanchored, so it also hides `app/newtab/` — the route the ecosystem page
# links to. Left as-is rather than changed here: this fix is for the build
# break, and un-ignoring it adds files that were never meant to be in the
# commit that repairs the deploy.
newtab/
!components/newtab/
!components/newtab/**

# Generated SQL bundles from scripts/bundle-work-migrations.mjs. The migrations
# in supabase-work/migrations/ are the source of truth; these are throwaway
# copies made for pasting into a SQL editor.
/apply-*.sql
/verify-*.sql
